UNIT f64_canonical_bits
LANG rust
PRIMARY-SHA256 3c0b7f98393070d57862813cef57df44b98940a1fb81567dc0cc6f17c633512d
SIG pub fn f64_canonical_bits(raw: &str) -> String
ABI crate shape: a LIBRARY crate (`[lib]`, `src/lib.rs` exporting `pub fn f64_canonical_bits`), no main, no I/O, zero
  dependencies. text=&str (UTF-8) in, String out. A throw (R1) is a panic; the verifier's harness catches it and records a
  throw observation, so the panic message is free.
MECHANISM R1 is NOT u64::from_str_radix alone: it accepts a leading '+' ("+fffffffffffffff" is 16 bytes and parses) and
  uppercase digits, and str::len counts UTF-8 bytes, not characters. Validate first: raw.len() == 16 and every byte is in
  b'0'..=b'9' or b'a'..=b'f' (u8::is_ascii_hexdigit alone also admits uppercase), else panic; only then from_str_radix(raw, 16).
MECHANISM R2-R4: let x = f64::from_bits(bits). from_bits and to_bits PRESERVE every NaN payload and sign, so without an explicit
  test a signalling or negative NaN comes back unchanged and fails the oracle. R3 = if x.is_nan() return QNAN (the pinned
  literal; do not rely on the bits of f64::NAN). R4 = format!("{:016x}", x.to_bits()), which equals raw for every non-NaN.
DEPENDS-ON none
PROVENANCE rederive@b15b56613f9d cli/sir/canon.mjs lines 8-13; Rust mapping per the v0.5 lab rust driver (&str in, String out).
